            <ArticleTitle>Headache secondary to an unusual Valsalva manoeuvre in a migrainous patient with cacosmia.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>103-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/s10072-011-0513-8</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We present the case of a 48-year old female patient with a history of migraine without aura, who also reported a headache triggered by a Valsalva manoeuvre, peculiarly performed with her head bent forward below her knees. By this position, she could obtain temporary relief from a persistent cacosmia of unknown etiology that had developed about 20 years earlier. A possible pathogenic mechanism for this headache is proposed.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
